    {"id": "hf_02", "condition": "heart_failure", "hop": "staging", "text": "Heart Failure Classification by Ejection Fraction and NYHA Class: HFrEF (reduced EF) defined as LVEF 40% or less. HFmrEF (mildly reduced) 41 to 49%. HFpEF (preserved) 50% or greater. NYHA Functional Classification: Class I - no limitation of physical activity; Class II - slight limitation, comfortable at rest; Class III - marked limitation, less than ordinary activity causes symptoms; Class IV - unable to carry out any physical activity without symptoms, symptoms at rest. ACC/AHA Stages A through D describe progression from at-risk to advanced heart failure."},
    {"id": "hf_03", "condition": "heart_failure", "hop": "acute_decompensation", "text": "Acute Decompensated Heart Failure (ADHF) Criteria: Rapid onset or worsening of heart failure symptoms requiring urgent therapy. Features include acute pulmonary edema, worsening dyspnea and orthopnea, weight gain from fluid retention, peripheral or pulmonary congestion, hypoxemia (SpO2 less than 90% on room air), and elevated natriuretic peptides. Warm/wet, cold/wet, warm/dry, and cold/dry hemodynamic profiles guide therapy, with cold/wet indicating cardiogenic shock requiring inotropes or mechanical support."},
    {"id": "hf_04", "condition": "heart_failure", "hop": "treatment", "text": "Guideline-Directed Medical Therapy (GDMT) for HFrEF per ACC/AHA/HFSA: Four foundational drug classes - ARNI (sacubitril/valsartan) or ACE inhibitor/ARB, evidence-based beta-blocker (carvedilol, metoprolol succinate, or bisoprolol), mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist (spironolactone or eplerenone), and SGLT2 inhibitor (dapagliflozin or empagliflozin). Loop diuretics (furosemide, torsemide, bumetanide) for volume overload. Titrate to target doses as tolerated. For acute decompensation: IV loop diuretics, oxygen if hypoxemic, and vasodilators if hypertensive."},
