    {"id": "afib_02", "condition": "atrial_fib", "hop": "stroke_risk", "text": "CHA2DS2-VASc Score for Stroke Risk in AFib: One point each for Congestive heart failure, Hypertension, Age 65 to 74, Diabetes mellitus, Vascular disease (prior MI, PAD, or aortic plaque), and female Sex category. Two points each for Age 75 or greater and prior Stroke/TIA/thromboembolism. Score 0 in men or 1 in women - no anticoagulation. Score 1 in men - consider anticoagulation. Score 2 or greater in men or 3 or greater in women - oral anticoagulation recommended."},
    {"id": "afib_03", "condition": "atrial_fib", "hop": "classification", "text": "AFib Classification: Paroxysmal - terminates spontaneously or with intervention within 7 days. Persistent - continuous for more than 7 days. Long-standing persistent - continuous for more than 12 months. Permanent - patient and clinician accept rhythm and rate control alone. Additional categories include valvular AFib (moderate-to-severe mitral stenosis or mechanical valve) which mandates warfarin, and non-valvular AFib. Rapid ventricular response is defined as ventricular rate greater than 110 bpm."},
    {"id": "afib_04", "condition": "atrial_fib", "hop": "treatment", "text": "AFib Management per 2023 ACC/AHA/ACCP/HRS: Two rhythm goals - rate control and/or rhythm control. Rate control agents: beta-blockers (metoprolol, atenolol), non-dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers (diltiazem, verapamil), or digoxin, targeting resting heart rate less than 110 bpm (lenient) or less than 80 bpm (strict). Rhythm control options: cardioversion (electrical or pharmacologic with amiodarone, flecainide, propafenone), antiarrhythmic drugs, or catheter ablation. Anticoagulation with DOACs (apixaban, rivaroxaban, dabigatran, edoxaban) preferred over warfarin for most non-valvular AFib. Warfarin required for valvular AFib with INR target 2 to 3."},
